/-!
# Uniform annihilation of the high Tor against the powers of an ideal

Let `A` be a Noetherian ring and `I ⊆ A` an ideal generated by at most `t` elements — the
Situation of [Stacks, Tag 0G9W], in which `p : X = Proj(⨁_{n ≥ 0} Iⁿ) ⟶ Spec A` is the blowup
of `Spec A` in `I` and `q(A, I)` is a threshold above which `Hⁱ(X, 𝒪_X(q)) = 0` for `i > 0` and
`H⁰(X, 𝒪_X(q)) = I^q`. [Stacks, Tag 0GA1] asserts that a single power of `I`, depending on `A`
and `I` alone and *not* on the module, annihilates `Tor_i^A(M, A/I^q)` for every `A`-module `M`,
every `i > t` and every `q ≥ 0`.

## What is formalized here, and what is assumed

The geometric half of the source's proof is carried as an explicit hypothesis. The source argues
that for `q ≥ q(A, I)` the hypercohomology spectral sequence of [Stacks, Tag 0G9Q], whose columns
vanish beyond `d = t` by [Stacks, Tag 071L], equips `Tor_i^A(M, I^q)` — identified with
`H^{-i}(X, Lp^*M̃(q))` by [Stacks, Tag 0G9Z] — with a finite filtration in at most `d` steps
whose graded pieces are subquotients of the `H^a(X, H^b(Lp^*M̃)(q))`, and those are annihilated
by `I^c` for `c = c(A, I)` by [Stacks, Tag 0GA0] as soon as `i ≥ t`. Assembling that chain in
Lean is not done here: the *existence of the filtration* is the hypothesis `hfilt` of
`Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.exists_pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow`, stated exactly
in the form the spectral sequence delivers it — a chain `⊤ = F 0, …, F d = ⊥` of submodules of
`Tor_i^A(M, I^q)` with `I^c • F k ≤ F (k + 1)`, for all `i ≥ t` and all `q` above a threshold.

Everything the source deduces from that filtration *is* proved here: that a filtration in `d`
steps with graded pieces killed by `I^c` forces `I^{dc}` to kill the whole module; that the short
exact sequence `0 → I^q → A → A/I^q → 0` transports the annihilation from `I^q` to `A/I^q` at the
cost of one degree, so that `i ≥ t` becomes `i > t`; that the finitely many `q` below the
threshold need no geometry at all, because `I^q` already annihilates `A/I^q` and hence every
`Tor_i^A(M, A/I^q)`; and that one exponent `m` therefore works for all `q ≥ 0` at once. The
degenerate case `t = 0`, where `I = (∅) = 0`, is proved outright and does not use `hfilt`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.pow_mul_le_annihilator_of_filtration`: a module with a
  filtration in `d` steps whose graded pieces are annihilated by `I^c` is annihilated by
  `I^{dc}`.
* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow`: `I^q` annihilates
  `Tor_i^A(M, A/I^q)`, in every degree and with no hypothesis on `q`.
* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.exists_pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 0GA1]**, modulo the hypothesis `hfilt` described above.
* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.exists_pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow_of_card_le_one`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 0GA1]** for `t ≤ 1`, with no hypothesis beyond that. For a principal ideal
  `I = (a)` the geometry is not needed: `I^q = (a^q)` is a cyclic module, so a single dimension
  shift replaces the filtration, and the exponent is an index at which the ascending chain of
  annihilators `ann(a) ⊆ ann(a²) ⊆ ⋯` stabilizes.

## Implementation notes

`Tor_i^A(M, N)` is `CategoryTheory.Tor'`, which derives the first variable: the source defines
`Tor_i^A(M, N)` as `H_i(F_• ⊗_A N)` for a free resolution `F_•` of `M`, and that is Mathlib's
`Tor'` rather than its `Tor`. The annihilator-propagation toolbox of
`StacksFrontier/RingTheory/Tor/AlmostFlatResolution.lean` and the long exact sequence it carries are
stated for `Tor`, whose *first* variable is the underived one; the two are matched by the
braiding isomorphism `torFlip_iso_Tor'`, which is the cheap half of the balancing of `Tor` and
needs no dimension shifting.

The upper bound `t` on the number of generators of `I` is presented as a generating family
`g : Fin t → A`, matching the convention of [Stacks, Tag 0GA0] in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Blowup/DerivedPullbackAnnihilation.lean`, where it appears as
`g : Fin (n + 1) → A` with `I = Ideal.span (Set.range g)`.

Annihilation is containment of a power of `I` in `Module.annihilator`, so the conclusion unfolds
to `∀ r ∈ I^m, ∀ x, r • x = 0`. The quantifier order `∃ m, ∀ M` is the whole content of the
source's lemma and is what is stated: the exponent is produced before the module is seen.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0GA1](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GA1)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0G9W](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0G9W)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0GA0](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GA0)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0G9Z](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0G9Z)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0G9Q](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0G9Q)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 071L](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/071L)
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

universe u

namespace Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII

variable {A : Type u} [CommRing A]

/-! ### Annihilation from a finite filtration -/

/-- **A module filtered in `d` steps with graded pieces annihilated by `Iᶜ` is annihilated by
`I^{dc}`.** The filtration is a family `F` of submodules with `F 0 = ⊤` and `F d = ⊥` such that
`Iᶜ • F k ≤ F (k + 1)`, which is annihilation of the graded piece `F k / F (k + 1)`. Multiplying
the `d` steps together, `I^{dc} • ⊤ ≤ F d = ⊥`. -/
theorem pow_mul_le_annihilator_of_filtration (I : Ideal A) (c d : ℕ) (T : Type*)
    [AddCommGroup T] [Module A T] (F : ℕ → Submodule A T) (h0 : F 0 = ⊤) (hd : F d = ⊥)
    (hstep : ∀ k, I ^ c • F k ≤ F (k + 1)) :
    I ^ (d * c) ≤ Module.annihilator A T := by
  have key : ∀ k, I ^ (k * c) • (⊤ : Submodule A T) ≤ F k := by
    intro k
    induction k with
    | zero => simp [h0]
    | succ k ih =>
        calc I ^ ((k + 1) * c) • (⊤ : Submodule A T)
            = I ^ c • (I ^ (k * c) • (⊤ : Submodule A T)) := by
              rw [show (k + 1) * c = c + k * c by ring, pow_add, ← Submodule.smul_assoc]
              congr 1
          _ ≤ I ^ c • F k := smul_mono_right _ ih
          _ ≤ F (k + 1) := hstep k
  intro r hr
  refine Module.mem_annihilator.mpr fun x => ?_
  have hx : r • x ∈ F d := key d (Submodule.smul_mem_smul hr Submodule.mem_top)
  rwa [hd, Submodule.mem_bot] at hx

/-! ### The two elementary inputs -/

/-- Every ring element annihilates a module that is a zero object. -/
theorem mem_annihilator_of_isZero {X : ModuleCat.{u} A} (hX : IsZero X) (r : A) :
    r ∈ Module.annihilator A X := by
  refine Module.mem_annihilator.mpr fun x => ?_
  have hx : x = 0 := by
    simpa using congrArg (fun f : X ⟶ X => f.hom x) (hX.eq_of_src (𝟙 X) 0)
  rw [hx, smul_zero]

/-- **`I^q` annihilates `Tor_i^A(M, A/I^q)`**, for every `A`-module `M`, every degree `i` and
every `q`: the underived variable `A/I^q` is annihilated by `I^q`, and annihilation of the
underived variable passes to `Tor`. This is what covers the values of `q` below the threshold of
[Stacks, Tag 0G9W] in the proof of [Stacks, Tag 0GA1]. -/
theorem pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow (I : Ideal A) (M : ModuleCat.{u} A) (i q : ℕ) :
    I ^ q ≤ Module.annihilator A
      (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) i).obj M).obj (ModuleCat.of A (A ⧸ I ^ q))) := by
  intro r hr
  have hquot : r ∈ Module.annihilator A (ModuleCat.of A (A ⧸ I ^ q)) := by
    refine Module.mem_annihilator.mpr fun x => ?_
    obtain ⟨y, rfl⟩ := Submodule.Quotient.mk_surjective (I ^ q) x
    exact (Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero (I ^ q)).mpr ((I ^ q).mul_mem_right y hr)
  exact mem_annihilator_of_moduleCat_iso (((torFlip_iso_Tor' A i).app M).app _)
    (mem_annihilator_tor_of_mem_annihilator _ M i hquot)

/-- Dimension shift along `0 → J → A → A ⧸ J → 0`: an element annihilating `Tor_j^A(M, J)` for
`j ≥ s` annihilates `Tor_i^A(M, N)` for `i > s`, whenever `N ≅ A ⧸ J`. -/
private theorem mem_annihilator_tor_of_iso_quotient_aux {N : ModuleCat.{u} A} {J : Ideal A}
    (e : ModuleCat.of A (A ⧸ J) ≅ N) (M : ModuleCat.{u} A) (s : ℕ) {r : A}
    (hr : ∀ j, s ≤ j → r ∈ Module.annihilator A
      (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) j).obj M).obj (ModuleCat.of A ↥J))) (i : ℕ) (hi : s < i) :
    r ∈ Module.annihilator A (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) i).obj M).obj N) := by
  have hshift := mem_annihilator_tor_of_shift
      (Stacks.Tor.DimensionShift.quotientShortComplex J)
      (Stacks.Tor.DimensionShift.shortExact_quotientShortComplex J) M
    (v := r) (u := 1) (m := s) (p := 1)
    (fun j hj => mem_annihilator_of_moduleCat_iso
      (((torFlip_iso_Tor' A j).app M).app (ModuleCat.of A ↥J)).symm (hr j hj))
    (fun k hk => by
      obtain ⟨k, rfl⟩ : ∃ k', k = k' + 1 := ⟨k - 1, by omega⟩
      exact mem_annihilator_of_isZero (isZero_Tor_succ_of_flat (ModuleCat.of A A) M k) 1)
    i (by omega) (by omega)
  rw [mul_one] at hshift
  exact mem_annihilator_of_moduleCat_iso (((torFlip_iso_Tor' A i).app M).app N)
    (mem_annihilator_of_moduleCat_iso (((Tor (ModuleCat.{u} A) i).mapIso e).app M) hshift)

/-- With no generators `I = 0`, so the exponent `1` works. -/
private theorem exists_pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow_of_card_zero {I : Ideal A}
    (g : Fin 0 → A) (hg : I = Ideal.span (Set.range g)) :
    ∃ m : ℕ, ∀ (M : ModuleCat.{u} A) (i q : ℕ), 0 < i →
      I ^ m ≤ Module.annihilator A
        (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) i).obj M).obj (ModuleCat.of A (A ⧸ I ^ q))) := by
  have hI : I = ⊥ := by rw [hg, Set.range_eq_empty, Ideal.span_empty]
  exact ⟨1, fun _ _ _ _ => by simp [hI]⟩

/-- **One degree of dimension shift along `0 → I^q → A → A/I^q → 0`.** An element annihilating
`Tor_j^A(M, I^q)` in all degrees `j ≥ s` annihilates `Tor_i^A(M, A/I^q)` in all degrees `i > s`,
because the middle term `A` of the sequence is flat and so has vanishing higher `Tor`. -/
theorem mem_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow_of_tor_pow (I : Ideal A) (M : ModuleCat.{u} A)
    (q s : ℕ) {r : A}
    (hr : ∀ j, s ≤ j → r ∈ Module.annihilator A
      (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) j).obj M).obj (ModuleCat.of A ↥(I ^ q)))) :
    ∀ i, s < i → r ∈ Module.annihilator A
      (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) i).obj M).obj (ModuleCat.of A (A ⧸ I ^ q))) := by
  exact mem_annihilator_tor_of_iso_quotient_aux (Iso.refl _) M s hr

/-! ### Stacks tag 0GA1 -/

/-- **Stacks Project, Tag 0GA1.** Let `A` be a Noetherian ring and let `I = (g 0, …, g (t-1))` be
an ideal of `A` generated by at most `t` elements, as in the Situation of [Stacks, Tag 0G9W].
There is an integer `m`, depending only on `A` and `I`, such that for *every* `A`-module `M` the
modules `Tor_i^A(M, A/I^q)` are annihilated by `I^m` for all `i > t` and all `q ≥ 0`.

The hypothesis `hfilt` is the geometric input of the source's proof, which is not formalized
here: for all `i ≥ t` and all `q ≥ q₀`, the module `Tor_i^A(M, I^q)` carries a filtration
`⊤ = F 0, …, F d = ⊥` in `d` steps whose graded pieces are annihilated by `Iᶜ`. The source
produces it from the hypercohomology spectral sequence [Stacks, Tag 0G9Q] of
`Lp^*M̃(q)` on the blowup `p : X ⟶ Spec A` of `Spec A` in `I`, with `q₀ = q(A, I)`, with `d = t`
by the vanishing [Stacks, Tag 071L] for the cover of `X` by the `t` affine charts, and with
`c = c(A, I)` the exponent of [Stacks, Tag 0GA0]; the identification of the abutment with
`Tor_i^A(M, I^q)` is [Stacks, Tag 0G9Z].

Given `hfilt`, the exponent `m = max (d * c) q₀ + 1` works: above the threshold the filtration
gives `I^{dc} • Tor_i^A(M, I^q) = 0` for `i ≥ t`, which the sequence `0 → I^q → A → A/I^q → 0`
shifts to `A/I^q` for `i > t`, and for the remaining `q ≤ m` already `I^m ≤ I^q` annihilates
`A/I^q` and hence `Tor_i^A(M, A/I^q)`. -/
@[stacks 0GA1]
theorem exists_pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow [IsNoetherianRing A] {I : Ideal A} {t : ℕ}
    (g : Fin t → A) (hg : I = Ideal.span (Set.range g)) (q₀ d c : ℕ)
    (hfilt : ∀ (M : ModuleCat.{u} A) (i q : ℕ), t ≤ i → q₀ ≤ q →
      ∃ F : ℕ → Submodule A
          (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) i).obj M).obj (ModuleCat.of A ↥(I ^ q))),
        F 0 = ⊤ ∧ F d = ⊥ ∧ ∀ k, I ^ c • F k ≤ F (k + 1)) :
    ∃ m : ℕ, ∀ (M : ModuleCat.{u} A) (i q : ℕ), t < i →
      I ^ m ≤ Module.annihilator A
        (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) i).obj M).obj (ModuleCat.of A (A ⧸ I ^ q))) := by
  rcases Nat.eq_zero_or_pos t with rfl | ht
  · exact exists_pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow_of_card_zero g hg
  refine ⟨max (d * c) q₀ + 1, fun M i q hi r hr => ?_⟩
  rcases le_or_gt q (max (d * c) q₀ + 1) with hq | hq
  · -- Below the threshold: `I^m ≤ I^q`, and `I^q` already annihilates `A/I^q`.
    exact pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow I M i q (Ideal.pow_le_pow_right hq hr)
  · -- Above the threshold: the filtration annihilates `Tor_j(M, I^q)` for `j ≥ t`, and the
    -- sequence `0 → I^q → A → A/I^q → 0` shifts that to `A/I^q` in degrees `> t`.
    refine mem_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow_of_tor_pow I M q t (fun j hj => ?_) i hi
    obtain ⟨F, hF0, hFd, hFstep⟩ := hfilt M j q hj (by omega)
    exact pow_mul_le_annihilator_of_filtration I c d _ F hF0 hFd hFstep
      (Ideal.pow_le_pow_right (by omega) hr)

/-! ### Stacks tag 0GA1 for an ideal generated by at most one element -/

/-- **Annihilation of the higher `Tor` against a cyclic module.** If `N ≅ A ⧸ J` for an ideal `J`
annihilated by `r`, then `r` annihilates `Tor_i^A(M, N)` for every `A`-module `M` and every
`i > 0`: the middle term of `0 → J → A → A ⧸ J → 0` is flat, so a single dimension shift carries
the annihilation of `J` to the higher `Tor` of the quotient. -/
theorem mem_annihilator_tor_of_iso_quotient {N : ModuleCat.{u} A} {J : Ideal A}
    (e : ModuleCat.of A (A ⧸ J) ≅ N) {r : A}
    (hr : r ∈ Module.annihilator A (ModuleCat.of A ↥J)) (M : ModuleCat.{u} A) (i : ℕ)
    (hi : 0 < i) :
    r ∈ Module.annihilator A (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) i).obj M).obj N) := by
  exact mem_annihilator_tor_of_iso_quotient_aux e M 0
    (fun j _ => mem_annihilator_of_moduleCat_iso
      (((torFlip_iso_Tor' A j).app M).app (ModuleCat.of A ↥J))
      (mem_annihilator_tor_of_mem_annihilator _ M j hr)) i hi

/-- **The higher `Tor` against a power of a principal ideal.** Let `I = (a)` and suppose that
every `x` with `x * a^q = 0` already satisfies `x * a^{q₀} = 0`. Then `a^{q₀}` annihilates
`Tor_i^A(M, I^q)` for every `A`-module `M` and every `i > 0`, because `I^q = (a^q)` is the cyclic
module `A ⧸ ann(a^q)` and its relation ideal `ann(a^q)` is annihilated by `a^{q₀}`. -/
theorem mem_annihilator_tor_pow_of_span_singleton {I : Ideal A} {a : A}
    (hI : I = Ideal.span {a}) {q₀ q : ℕ} (hstab : ∀ x : A, x * a ^ q = 0 → x * a ^ q₀ = 0)
    (M : ModuleCat.{u} A) (i : ℕ) (hi : 0 < i) :
    a ^ q₀ ∈ Module.annihilator A
      (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) i).obj M).obj (ModuleCat.of A ↥(I ^ q))) := by
  have hmem : ∀ x : A, LinearMap.toSpanSingleton A A (a ^ q) x ∈ I ^ q := by
    intro x
    rw [hI, Ideal.span_singleton_pow]
    exact Ideal.mem_span_singleton'.mpr ⟨x, rfl⟩
  set φ : A →ₗ[A] ↥(I ^ q) :=
    LinearMap.codRestrict _ (LinearMap.toSpanSingleton A A (a ^ q)) hmem with hφ
  have hφapp : ∀ x : A, (φ x : A) = x * a ^ q :=
    fun x => by simp [hφ, LinearMap.toSpanSingleton_apply]
  have hsurj : Function.Surjective φ := by
    intro y
    have hy : (y : A) ∈ Ideal.span {a ^ q} := by
      rw [← Ideal.span_singleton_pow, ← hI]
      exact y.2
    obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := Ideal.mem_span_singleton'.mp hy
    exact ⟨x, Subtype.ext (by rw [hφapp, hx])⟩
  have hann : a ^ q₀ ∈ Module.annihilator A (ModuleCat.of A ↥(LinearMap.ker φ)) := by
    refine Module.mem_annihilator.mpr fun x => ?_
    have hx : (x : A) * a ^ q = 0 := by
      rw [← hφapp]
      exact congrArg Subtype.val (LinearMap.mem_ker.mp x.2)
    exact Subtype.ext (by
      show a ^ q₀ * (x : A) = 0
      rw [mul_comm]
      exact hstab _ hx)
  exact mem_annihilator_tor_of_iso_quotient
    (LinearMap.quotKerEquivOfSurjective φ hsurj).toModuleIso hann M i hi

/-- **Stacks Project, Tag 0GA1 for an ideal generated by at most one element.** Let `A` be a
Noetherian ring and let `I = (g 0, …, g (t-1))` be an ideal generated by at most `t ≤ 1` elements.
There is an integer `m`, depending only on `A` and `I`, such that for *every* `A`-module `M` the
modules `Tor_i^A(M, A/I^q)` are annihilated by `I^m` for all `i > t` and all `q ≥ 0`. Nothing is
assumed: the geometric input of `exists_pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow` is not needed here.

For `t = 1` and `I = (a)` take `m = q₀`, an index at which the ascending chain of annihilators
`ann(a) ⊆ ann(a²) ⊆ ⋯` stabilizes. For `q ≤ q₀` already `I^{q₀} ≤ I^q` annihilates `A/I^q`. For
`q > q₀` the ideal `I^q = (a^q)` is the cyclic module `A ⧸ ann(a^q)` with `ann(a^q) = ann(a^{q₀})`
annihilated by `a^{q₀}`, so one dimension shift gives `a^{q₀} · Tor_i^A(M, I^q) = 0` for `i ≥ 1`
and a second, along `0 → I^q → A → A/I^q → 0`, gives the claim for `i > 1`. The case `t = 0`,
where `I = 0`, is the degenerate case of `exists_pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow`. -/
@[stacks 0GA1 "for an ideal generated by at most one element"]
theorem exists_pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow_of_card_le_one [IsNoetherianRing A]
    {I : Ideal A} {t : ℕ} (g : Fin t → A) (hg : I = Ideal.span (Set.range g)) (ht : t ≤ 1) :
    ∃ m : ℕ, ∀ (M : ModuleCat.{u} A) (i q : ℕ), t < i →
      I ^ m ≤ Module.annihilator A
        (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) i).obj M).obj (ModuleCat.of A (A ⧸ I ^ q))) := by
  obtain rfl | rfl : t = 0 ∨ t = 1 := by omega
  · exact exists_pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow_of_card_zero g hg
  · have hI : I = Ideal.span {g 0} := by
      rw [hg]
      congr 1
      ext x
      simp only [Set.mem_range, Set.mem_singleton_iff]
      refine ⟨fun h => ?_, fun h => ⟨0, h.symm⟩⟩
      obtain ⟨j, hj⟩ := h
      rw [Subsingleton.elim j 0] at hj
      exact hj.symm
    -- The ascending chain of annihilators of the powers of the generator.
    set F : ℕ →o Submodule A A :=
      ⟨fun n => LinearMap.ker (LinearMap.toSpanSingleton A A (g 0 ^ n)), by
        intro m n hmn x hx
        simp only [LinearMap.mem_ker, LinearMap.toSpanSingleton_apply, smul_eq_mul] at hx ⊢
        obtain ⟨k, rfl⟩ := Nat.exists_eq_add_of_le hmn
        rw [pow_add, ← mul_assoc, hx, zero_mul]⟩
    have hFapp : ∀ n, F n = LinearMap.ker (LinearMap.toSpanSingleton A A (g 0 ^ n)) :=
      fun _ => rfl
    obtain ⟨q₀, hq₀⟩ := monotone_stabilizes_iff_noetherian.mpr inferInstance F
    have hstab : ∀ q, q₀ ≤ q → ∀ x : A, x * g 0 ^ q = 0 → x * g 0 ^ q₀ = 0 := by
      intro q hq x hx
      have hmem : x ∈ F q := by
        rw [hFapp]
        simpa [LinearMap.mem_ker, LinearMap.toSpanSingleton_apply] using hx
      rw [← hq₀ q hq, hFapp] at hmem
      simpa [LinearMap.mem_ker, LinearMap.toSpanSingleton_apply] using hmem
    refine ⟨q₀, fun M i q hi => ?_⟩
    rcases le_or_gt q q₀ with hq | hq
    · -- Below the threshold: `I^{q₀} ≤ I^q`, and `I^q` already annihilates `A/I^q`.
      exact (Ideal.pow_le_pow_right hq).trans (pow_le_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow I M i q)
    · -- Above the threshold: `I^q` is cyclic with relation ideal annihilated by `a^{q₀}`, and the
      -- sequence `0 → I^q → A → A/I^q → 0` shifts that to `A/I^q` in degrees `> 1`.
      intro r hr
      refine mem_annihilator_tor_quotient_pow_of_tor_pow I M q 1 (fun j hj => ?_) i hi
      have hle : I ^ q₀ ≤ Module.annihilator A
          (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) j).obj M).obj (ModuleCat.of A ↥(I ^ q))) := by
        rw [show I ^ q₀ = Ideal.span {g 0 ^ q₀} by rw [hI, Ideal.span_singleton_pow],
          Ideal.span_le, Set.singleton_subset_iff]
        exact mem_annihilator_tor_pow_of_span_singleton hI (hstab q hq.le) M j (by omega)
      exact hle hr

end Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII

